import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
title: "Design Principles";
description: "Core design principles behind Umi‑Time.";
---

<Base title="Design Principles: Umi‑Time">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <main class="page page-design-principles">
      <header class="page-header">
        <h1>Umi‑Time Design Principles</h1>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time is built on the idea that the entire planet shares one
          continuous daily cycle. These principles define how the system works,
          how it looks, and why its interface behaves the way it does.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>1. Global First, Local Second</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time begins with the global perspective. The underlying time value
          is the same everywhere on Earth, derived directly from UTC with no
          offsets, regions, or seasonal changes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Local meaning is layered on top of this foundation. Your location
          affects the solar‑day context, but never the time itself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>2. One Day, One Rotation</h2>
        <p>
          The day is represented as a single continuous 360‑umi sweep. The hand
          completes one full rotation per UTC day, creating a calm, intuitive
          sense of the daily cycle.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>3. UTC as the Anchor</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time inherits the stability, precision, and universality of UTC.
          There are no time zones, daylight‑saving rules, or political offsets.
          UTC is the backbone of global systems; Umi‑Time builds directly on
          that foundation.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>4. Solar Context as a Fixed Layer</h2>
        <p>
          The solar ring is a static map of your solar day. It shows sunrise,
          sunset, twilight phases, solar noon, solar midnight, and day length,
          computed from your latitude and longitude. It provides local meaning
          without altering the global time value.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>5. Dial Orientation Reflects Longitude</h2>
        <p>
          The dial contains the 0ᵘ–360ᵘ UT scale and rotates only when the
          observer’s longitude changes. This ensures Umi‑Time noon is at the top
          and Umi‑Time midnight at the bottom, giving each location a consistent
          orientation for interpreting the universal time value.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>6. The Hand Is the Only Moving Element</h2>
        <p>
          The hand is the dynamic core of the interface. It rotates
          continuously—one sweep per UTC day—and simultaneously points to
          Umi‑Time on the dial and to the solar‑day phase on the ring. Keeping
          everything else static preserves clarity and calmness.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>7. Geometry and Numerics in Balance</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time supports both geometric and numeric representations. Geometry
          makes the daily cycle readable at a glance, while the Specification
          defines precise numeric formats for Umi‑Time and related values. Both
          forms are equally valid and intentionally complementary.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>8. Minimalism and Non‑Interference</h2>
        <p>
          The interface avoids clutter and unnecessary decoration. The dial is
          clean, the solar ring is subtle, and the hand is the only animated
          element. This restraint keeps the clock focused on the structure of
          the day.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>9. Universality and Accessibility</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time must work everywhere: at any latitude, in any season, with or
          without location services, online or offline, and across cultures and
          languages. The design avoids assumptions tied to specific regions,
          calendars, or traditions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>10. Predictability and Transparency</h2>
        <p>
          The system behaves in a fully transparent way. The hand moves at a
          constant rate, the dial orientation is determined solely by longitude,
          and the solar ring is computed from clear, published formulas with no
          hidden adjustments. Predictability builds trust.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>11. Separation of Concerns</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time maintains strict conceptual boundaries: Umi‑Time is pure
          global time, solar geometry is pure local context, and civil time is a
          separate system entirely. This separation allows Umi‑Time to coexist
          cleanly with existing time standards.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle">
        <h2>12. Grounded in Physical Reality</h2>
        <p>
          Civil time is shaped by politics; UTC is shaped by atomic clocks;
          Umi‑Time is shaped by UTC. Its representation of the day is continuous
          and universal, while the solar ring provides the physical context of
          the observer’s solar day.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="principle principle-conclusion">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time unifies global precision, local meaning, and intuitive
          geometry into a single universal and local representation of the UTC day. These
          design principles ensure that Umi‑Time remains simple, consistent, and
          locally meaningful — a time‑keeping system built for a connected
          planet.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
